   "title": "OPay’s $19 US IPO Faces Setback as US Investors Question Valuation, Nigerians Compare Dangote’s Share Offer OPay’s planned $304 million init",
   "url": "https://x.com/NaijaTruths/status/2116767448540078448",
   "snippet": "OPay’s $19 US IPO Faces Setback as US Investors Question Valuation, Nigerians Compare Dangote’s Share Offer\n\nOPay’s planned $304 million initial public offering (IPO) in the United States has suffered a major setback after indications emerged that investors were unwilling to meet its proposed $19-per-share price.\n\nThe Chinese-backed Nigerian fintech company had planned to offer approximately 16 million American Depositary Shares (ADSs) on the New York Stock Exchange, allowing American investors to acquire stakes without directly accessing Nigeria’s stock market.\n\nHowever, reports indicated that prospective investors were offering between $16.25 and $16.75 per share, considerably below the proposed $19 price.\n\nOPay subsequently suspended the offering, citing an outstanding regulatory inquiry in an undisclosed foreign jurisdiction.\n\nThe development has triggered discussions among Nigerians, with many comparing OPay’s proposed dollar-denominated share offer with Dangote Refinery’s share offering in Nigeria.\n\nWhile Dangote's shares were offered at ₦525 each, OPay's proposed $19 price translated to approximately ₦25,650 at an exchange rate of ₦1,350 to the dollar.\n\nSome commentators have questioned whether OPay overestimated its valuation by targeting American investors instead of testing demand in its home market.\n\nThe setback has also reignited conversations about corporate valuation, investor confidence and the importance of understanding market expectations before launching a public offering.\n\nOPay has not confirmed that pricing concerns caused the suspension.",
   "body": "OPay’s $19 US IPO Faces Setback as US Investors Question Valuation, Nigerians Compare Dangote’s Share Offer\n\nOPay’s planned $304 million initial public offering (IPO) in the United States has suffered a major setback after indications emerged that investors were unwilling to meet its proposed $19-per-share price.\n\nThe Chinese-backed Nigerian fintech company had planned to offer approximately 16 million American Depositary Shares (ADSs) on the New York Stock Exchange, allowing American investors to acquire stakes without directly accessing Nigeria’s stock market.\n\nHowever, reports indicated that prospective investors were offering between $16.25 and $16.75 per share, considerably below the proposed $19 price.\n\nOPay subsequently suspended the offering, citing an outstanding regulatory inquiry in an undisclosed foreign jurisdiction.\n\nThe development has triggered discussions among Nigerians, with many comparing OPay’s proposed dollar-denominated share offer with Dangote Refinery’s share offering in Nigeria.\n\nWhile Dangote's shares were offered at ₦525 each, OPay's proposed $19 price translated to approximately ₦25,650 at an exchange rate of ₦1,350 to the dollar.\n\nSome commentators have questioned whether OPay overestimated its valuation by targeting American investors instead of testing demand in its home market.\n\nThe setback has also reignited conversations about corporate valuation, investor confidence and the importance of understanding market expectations before launching a public offering.\n\nOPay has not confirmed that pricing concerns caused the suspension.",
